yword\\\": \\\"WHEN\\\"}, {\\\"content\\\": \\\"The system enters the “owner/repository name” overview page, marked Private; the default branch contains a README file, and the commit history contains one initialization commit; the repository also appears in the target owner's repository list. Seed values: public repository `acme-docs`, private repository `secret-research`.\\\", \\\"keyword\\\": \\\"THEN\\\"}, {\\\"content\\\": \\\"After refreshing the overview page, the owner, visibility, README, and initialization commit remain; if the name is empty or duplicated, the user lacks organization-creation permission, or any step fails during creation, the system displays the reason and leaves no partially created repository. Seed values: public repository `acme-docs`, private repository `secret-research`.\\\", \\\"keyword\\\": \\\"THEN\\\"}]}, {\\\"name\\\": \\\"REQ-3-2-1: Create a Repository with Owner, Visibility, and Initialization Options - Scenario 2\\\", \\\"steps\\\": [{\\\"content\\\": \\\"The visitor starts at the application home page in a fresh unauthenticated browser session. The pre-provisioned `repo-owner` (`repo-owner@example.test`, password `Valid-password-123!`) owns the personal namespace. The repository `acme-docs` already exists in that namespace.\\\", \\\"keyword\\\": \\\"GIVEN\\\"}, {\\\"content\\\": \\\"The visitor signs in as `repo-owner`, opens “New repository”, enters the existing repository name `acme-docs`, and clicks “Create repository”.\\\", \\\"keyword\\\": \\\"WHEN\\\"}, {\\\"content\\\": \\\"The page displays the repository-name conflict message and does not display the existing repository overview heading.\\\", \\\"keyword\\\": \\\"THEN\\\"}]}, {\\\"name\\\": \\\"REQ-3-2-1: Create a Repository with Owner, Visibility, and Initialization Options - Scenario 3\\\", \\\"steps\\\": [{\\\"content\\\": \\\"The visitor starts at the application home page in a fresh unauthenticated browser session. The pre-provisioned `repo-owner` (`repo-owner@example.test`, password `Valid-password-123!`) owns the personal namespace.\\\", \\\"keyword\\\": \\\"GIVEN\\\"}, {\\\"content\\\": \\\"The visitor signs in as `repo-owner`, opens “New repository”, leaves “Repository name” empty, and clicks “Create repository”.\\\", \\\"keyword\\\": \\\"WHEN\\\"}, {\\\"content\\\": \\\"The page displays a repository-name-required validation message.\\\", \\\"keyword\\\": \\\"THEN\\\"}]}], \\\"type\\\": \\\"ATOMIC\\\"}, \\\"effective_visual_references\\\": [], \\\"folder_context\\\": [\\\"{\\\\\\\"dependencies\\\\\\\": [], \\\\\\\"description\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"This product is a simplified GitHub collaboration platform. Unless an atomic requirement explicitly allows more than one role, every quoted user-interface name is the exact English accessible name of the control; the role, scope, and observable-state rules stated by each atomic requirement are authoritative. Values described as existing accounts, organizations, teams, repositories, branches, files, commits, issues, milestones, pull requests, reviews, and permission relationships are predefined seed data. The application must provision those records before the corresponding scenario and supply each account through an isolated browser session; they are not extra features that a participant must expose through a private API. Generated names may contain a unique suffix, but their role, ownership, visibility, and relationships are exactly those stated in the atomic requirement. Every atomic requirement remains enabled even when seed configuration is incomplete. Organization Owner, repository Admin, Read, Triage, Write, Maintain, and Admin permissions follow the operation-specific rules in the requirements below; these roles are not an automatic cumulative ladder. Real-time collaboration, Actions, Packages, Wiki, project boards, notification delivery, and external Git remote protocols are outside the scope of this product.\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"ROOT\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"GitHub - Stage 2\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"FOLDER\\\\\\\"}\\\", \\\"{\\\\\\\"dependencies\\\\\\\": [], \\\\\\\"description\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"A repository is a container for code, branches, commits, files, and collaborative work items. The repository overview page is titled “owner/repository name”, displays the owner, name, Public/Private marker, description, and default branch, and provides Code, Issues, Pull requests, and Settings entries; the “owner” is an individual account or organization, and a repository belonging to an organization is an organization repository. Users open a repository overview from global search results, a personal/organization Repositories list, or a direct link; signed-in users with creation permission create a personal or organization repository from “New repository”. Public repositories allow visitor Read access; private personal repositories allow access only to their owner and explicitly authorized subjects; private organization repositories allow access only to organization Owners, members with direct repository roles, or direct members of teams with repository roles, and ordinary organization Members do not automatically gain access merely through membership. After visibility or access grants change, search, lists, direct links, and repository pages must all use the same access rule.\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"REQ-3\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Repository Asset Management\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"FOLDER\\\\\\\"}\\\", \\\"{\\\\\\\"dependencies\\\\\\\": [\\\\\\\"REQ-1-1-2\\\\\\\"], \\\\\\\"description\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"The repository-creation page is the form opened by “New repository”, where the user selects an owner, name, visibility, and initialization options. A “fork” is an independent repository copied from a readable source repository — the frontend displays the source link on the fork overview page, and the system stores the source-repository identifier; branches and commits in the fork may evolve independently and never write back to the source repository. A “clone page entry” is a read-only address displayed in the repository Code popover and can be copied to the clipboard; it does not mean this product implements an external Git protocol. Both new repositories and forks must enter a browsable repository overview page. This feature builds on the persisted objects, opened pages, or authenticated-session context provided by REQ-1-1-2 (Sign In with an Existing Account); it must reuse that context and keep pages and data consistent after the context changes.\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"REQ-3-2\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Repository Creation and Distribution\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"FOLDER\\\\\\\"}\\\"], \\\"requirement_id\\\": \\\"REQ-3-2-1\\\"}\", \"REQ-3-2-2\": \"{\\\"atomic_requirement\\\": {\\\"dependencies\\\": [\\\"REQ-3-3\\\", \\\"REQ-1-1-2\\\"], \\\"description\\\": \\\"The source overview has a button “Fork”. The fork form contains a field labeled “Repository name” and button “Create fork”; it defaults to the signed-in user's personal namespace and an allowed visibility, permitting submission after editing only the name. Seed data supplies a readable source, a user allowed to create personal repositories, and an existing fork name in that namespace for the conflict case. Success opens a heading containing the new fork name and visible text “Forked from \\u003csource repository name>” with a source link; the heading and source relationship survive reload. On the source repository page, the user clicks “Fork”, selects a target personal or organization namespace, enters an optional new name and visibility, and clicks “Create fork”. The system creates an independent fork, copies the accessible default-branch history, records the source-repository link, and then opens the new fork overview page. This feature builds on the persisted objects, opened pages, or authenticated-session context provided by REQ-3-3 (View a Public Repository Overview) and REQ-1-1-2 (Sign In with an Existing Account); it must reuse that context and keep pages and data consistent after the context changes. A signed-in user can fork only when they have Read or higher permission on the source repository and permission to create repositories in the target personal or organization namespace; when the source repository is private, the fork must remain Private, while a public source repository may be forked as Public or Private. The system validates permissions on both ends and stores the new fork's owner, visibility, source-repository identifier, default branch, and copied accessible history; if any check fails, no fork is created.\\\\n\\\\nThe fork conflict seed uses the existing name `acme-docs-fork` in the target\\\\npersonal namespace.\\\\n\\\\nScreenshot reference:\\\\n![image](reference/github-fork-repository.png)\\\", \\\"id\\\": \\\"REQ-3-2-2\\\", \\\"name\\\": \\\"Fork a Repository into Another Namespace\\\", \\\"scenarios\\\": [{\\\"name\\\": \\\"REQ-3-2-2: Fork a Repository into Another Namespace - Scenario 1\\\", \\\"steps\\\": [{\\\"content\\\": \\\"The visitor starts at the application home page in a fresh unauthenticated browser session. The system pre-provisions the verified account `fork-user` (`fork-user@example.test`, password `Valid-password-123!`) with permission to read public repository `acme-docs` and create repositories in its personal namespace. No fork with the generated test name exists in that namespace.\\\", \\\"keyword\\\": \\\"GIVEN\\\"}, {\\\"content\\\": \\\"The user clicks “Fork”, selects the target namespace, keeps or enters a valid new repository name and optional visibility, then clicks “Create fork”. Seed values: public repository `acme-docs`, private repository `secret-research`.\\\", \\\"keyword\\\": \\\"WHEN\\\"}, {\\\"content\\\": \\\"The system opens the target “owner/fork name” overview page, which displays the source repository of the fork; 