icitly authorized subjects; private organization repositories allow access only to organization Owners, members with direct repository roles, or direct members of teams with repository roles, and ordinary organization Members do not automatically gain access merely through membership. After visibility or access grants change, search, lists, direct links, and repository pages must all use the same access rule.\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"REQ-3\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Repository Asset Management\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"FOLDER\\\\\\\"}\\\", \\\"{\\\\\\\"dependencies\\\\\\\": [\\\\\\\"REQ-1-1-2\\\\\\\"], \\\\\\\"description\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"The repository-creation page is the form opened by “New repository”, where the user selects an owner, name, visibility, and initialization options. A “fork” is an independent repository copied from a readable source repository — the frontend displays the source link on the fork overview page, and the system stores the source-repository identifier; branches and commits in the fork may evolve independently and never write back to the source repository. A “clone page entry” is a read-only address displayed in the repository Code popover and can be copied to the clipboard; it does not mean this product implements an external Git protocol. Both new repositories and forks must enter a browsable repository overview page. This feature builds on the persisted objects, opened pages, or authenticated-session context provided by REQ-1-1-2 (Sign In with an Existing Account); it must reuse that context and keep pages and data consistent after the context changes.\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"REQ-3-2\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Repository Creation and Distribution\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"FOLDER\\\\\\\"}\\\"], \\\"requirement_id\\\": \\\"REQ-3-2-2\\\"}\", \"REQ-3-3\": \"{\\\"atomic_requirement\\\": {\\\"dependencies\\\": [], \\\"description\\\": \\\"The repository overview iden