": \"ROOT\", \"name\": \"GitHub - Stage 2\", \"type\": \"FOLDER\"}\n{\"dependencies\": [], \"description\": \"A repository is a container for code, branches, commits, files, and collaborative work items. The repository overview page is titled “owner/repository name”, displays the owner, name, Public/Private marker, description, and default branch, and provides Code, Issues, Pull requests, and Settings entries; the “owner” is an individual account or organization, and a repository belonging to an organization is an organization repository. Users open a repository overview from global search results, a personal/organization Repositories list, or a direct link; signed-in users with creation permission create a personal or organization repository from “New repository”. Public repositories allow visitor Read access; private personal repositories allow access only to their owner and explicitly authorized subjects; private organization repositories allow access only to organization Owners, members with direct repository roles, or direct members of teams with repository roles, and ordinary organization Members do not automatically gain access merely through membership. After visibility or access grants change, search, lists, direct links, and repository pages must all use the same access rule.\", \"id\": \"REQ-3\", \"name\": \"Repository Asset Management\", \"type\": \"FOLDER\"}\n{\"dependencies\": [\"REQ-1-1-2\"], \"description\": \"The repository-creation page is the form opened by “New repository”, where the user selects an owner, name, visibility, and initialization options. A “fork” is an independent repository copied from a readable source repository — the frontend displays the source link on the fork overview page, and the system stores the source-repository identifier; branches and commits in the fork may evolve independently and never write back to the source repository. A “clone page entry” is a read-only address displayed in the repository Code popover and can be