1-2 (Sign In with an Existing Account); it must reuse that context and keep pages and data consistent after the context changes. The signed-in workspace provides a “New repository” link. The form labels are “Owner”, “Repository name”, and “Description”; the signed-in user's personal namespace is selected by default, so it is possible to submit without changing the owner. Visibility choices are radio controls “Public” and “Private”; initialization is a checkbox “Add a README file”; submission uses the button “Create repository”. Other defaults must allow duplicate-name and empty-name validation without further input. A successful initialized private repository shows a heading containing the new name, a visible Private marker, and a README file link; the overview persists after reload. The optional description “Repository created by Playwright” is saved when entered. An existing repository in this same default personal namespace is supplied for duplicate validation, which stays on the form and does not open the existing repository heading. On the repository-creation page, a signed-in user selects a personal or organization owner, enters a repository name and description, selects public or private visibility, and chooses whether to create an initial commit with a README. The system verifies that the user may create a repository in that owner's namespace, that the name is unique, and that the options are valid; on success, it stores the repository identifier, owner, visibility, default branch, and creator. If initialization is selected, it must also atomically create the initial branch, README file, and initial commit; failure at any step must leave no partially created repository. On success, the new repository overview page is opened.\\\\n\\\\nScreenshot reference:\\\\n![image](reference/github-create-repository.png)\\\", \\\"id\\\": \\\"REQ-3-2-1\\\", \\\"name\\\": \\\"Create a Repository with Owner, Visibility, and Initialization Options\\\", \\\"scenarios\\\": [{\\\"name\\\": \\\"REQ-3-2-1: Create a Repository with Owner, Visibility, and Initialization Options - Scenario 1\\\", \\\"steps\\\": [{\\\"content\\\": \\\"The visitor starts at the application home page in a fresh unauthenticated browser session. The pre-provisioned `repo-owner` (`repo-owner@example.test`, password `Valid-password-123!`) owns the personal namespace and may create repositories there. The existing repository `acme-docs` is available for duplicate-name validation, while the candidate repository name is unused. the creation page from “New repository”; the personal namespace is selectable. If an organization namespace is selected, the user has permission to create repositories for that organization, and the candidate repository name is unused in the target namespace.\\\", \\\"keyword\\\": \\\"GIVEN\\\"}, {\\\"content\\\": \\\"The user selects the target owner, enters a valid and unique repository name and optional description, selects “Private”, checks “Add a README file”, and clicks “Create repository”. Seed values: public repository `acme-docs`, private repository `secret-research`.\\\", \\\"keyword\\\": \\\"WHEN\\\"}, {\\\"content\\\": \\\"The system enters the “owner/repository name” overview page, marked Private; the default branch contains a README file, and the commit history contains one initialization commit; the repository also appears in the target owner's repository list. Seed values: public repository `acme-docs`, private repository `secret-research`.\\\", \\\"keyword\\\": \\\"THEN\\\"}, {\\\"content\\\": \\\"After refreshing the overview page, the owner, visibility, README, and initialization commit remain; if the name is empty or duplicated, the user lacks organization-creation permission, or any step fails during creation, the system displays the reason and leaves no partially created repository. Seed values: public repository `acme-docs`, private repository `secret-research`.\\\", \\\"keyword\\\": \\\"THEN\\\"}]}, {\\\"name\\\": \\\"REQ-3