ccess; private personal repositories allow access only to their owner and explicitly authorized subjects; private organization repositories allow access only to organization Owners, members with direct repository roles, or direct members of teams with repository roles, and ordinary organization Members do not automatically gain access merely through membership. After visibility or access grants change, search, lists, direct links, and repository pages must all use the same access rule.\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"REQ-3\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Repository Asset Management\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"FOLDER\\\\\\\"}\\\", \\\"{\\\\\\\"dependencies\\\\\\\": [\\\\\\\"REQ-1-1-2\\\\\\\"], \\\\\\\"description\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"The repository-creation page is the form opened by “New repository”, where the user selects an owner, name, visibility, and initialization options. A “fork” is an independent repository copied from a readable source repository — the frontend displays the source link on the fork overview page, and the system stores the source-repository identifier; branches and commits in the fork may evolve independently and never write back to the source repository. A “clone page entry” is a read-only address displayed in the repository Code popover and can be copied to the clipboard; it does not mean this product implements an external Git protocol. Both new repositories and forks must enter a browsable repository overview page. This feature builds on the persisted objects, opened pages, or authenticated-session context provided by REQ-1-1-2 (Sign In with an Existing Account); it must reuse that context and keep pages and data consistent after the context changes.\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"REQ-3-2\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Repository Creation and Distribution\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"FOLDER\\\\\\\"}\\\"], \\\"requirement_id\\\": \\\"REQ-3-2-1\\\"}\", \"REQ-3-2-2\": \"{\\\"atomic_requirement\\\": {\\\"dependencies\\\": [\\\"REQ-3-3\\\", \\\"REQ-1-1-2\\\"], \\\"description\\\": \\\"The source overview has a button “Fork”. The fork form contains a field labeled “Repository name” and button “Create fork”; it defaults to the signed-in user's personal namespace and an allowed visibility, permitting submission after editing only the name. Seed data supplies a readable source, a user allowed to create personal repositories, and an existing fork name in that namespace for the conflict case. Success opens a heading containing the new fork name and visible text “Forked from \\u003csource repository name>” with a source link; the heading and source relationship survive reload. On the source repository page, the user clicks “Fork”, selects a target personal or organization namespace, enters an optional new name and visibility, and clicks “Create fork”. The system creates an independent fork, copies the accessible default-branch history, records the source-repository link, and then opens the new fork overview page. This feature builds on the persisted objects, opened pages, or authenticated-session context provided by REQ-3-3 (View a Public Repository Overview) and REQ-1-1-2 (Sign In with an Existing Account); it must reuse that context and keep pages and data consistent after the context changes. A signed-in user can fork only when they have Read or higher permission on the source repository and permission to create repositories in the target personal or organization namespace; when the source repository is private, the fork must remain Private, while a public source repository may be forked as Public or Private. The system validates permissions on both ends and stores the new fork's owner, visibility, source-repository identifier, default branch, and copied accessible history; if any check fails, no fork is created.\\\\n\\\\nThe fork conflict seed uses the existing name `acme-docs-fork` in the target\\\\npersonal namespace.\\\\n\\\\nScreenshot reference:\\\\n![image](r